\DOC X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> term list -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic x86 {eventually} execution goal by one machine instruction,
replacing the current state variable throughout the goal and assumptions by a
fresh successor state.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {th : thm * (thm option array)} pairs the code-length
theorem for the loaded program with an array of (optional) per-instruction
decoding theorems used to step the execution; {pats : term list} is a list of
term patterns selecting which state components are carried over/retained
symbolically for the new state; {s : string} names the new state variable to
introduce for the state after the step. The goal must have an {eventually}
conclusion about a current state variable, with assumptions including a
program-loaded fact, a program-counter fact, a {MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state to the current state, and further state-indexed reads. All of
these are re-expressed for the new state named by {s}: the state-dependent
assumptions are removed and replaced by the corresponding assumptions about the
new state, with the program-counter assumption's offset advanced past the
executed instruction, while assumptions not mentioning the state (such as
nonoverlapping conditions) are left untouched; the conclusion is the same
{eventually} statement applied to the new state (written {t'} below).
{
       A, read PC t = u, R t ?- eventually ... t
   =============================================  X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC th pats s
      A, read PC t' = u', R t' ?- eventually ... t'
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals of this x86 symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
